export const SEARCH_INDEX = [
  {"u":"docs.html","t":"Language reference","p":"Docs","l":1,"x":"Everything in the language, in the order you would meet it. This page follows the formal definition, which is the authority where the two disagree. How to read the grammars { x } is zero or more, [ x ] is optional, and | separates alternatives. Words in this colour are literal; this one is a placeholder. Round and square brackets are interchangeable as long as they match — square brackets are used by convention where a form takes a list of clauses."},
  {"u":"docs.html#lexical","t":"Lexical structure","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"A semicolon begins a comment that runs to the end of the line. Each bracket is a token on its own. A string is \"…\" with the escapes \\\", \\\\ and \\n. Every other character joins a token that extends as far as it can, stopping only at a bracket, a semicolon, whitespace or a quote. Token Form Examples Meaning numeral digits, optional sign 42 -7 a 64-bit integer money $ digits, optionally . and up to six more $0.50 $20 integer micro-dollars; $0.50 is 500 000 µ$ duration digits then ms, s, min or h 250ms 30s 10min integer milliseconds Boolean #t or #f symbol ' then a name 'declined a symbol value empty list '() name starts lowercase or with a symbol character react best-of + a variable or function Name starts uppercase Step Exec Text a constructor, record or type Money and time are not numbers They are distinct kinds of value. You may add money to money, and a duration to a duration, but never money to seconds, and never either to a Num."},
  {"u":"docs.html#program","t":"Program structure","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"A file is a sequence of definitions and extended definitions. Definitions are the language proper. Extended definitions arrange the world around it: loading another file, receiving authority from the host, and testing. As in Ramsey's interpreters, a file's unit tests run after the whole file has loaded, so a test may mention anything the file defines, in any order. def ::= (val name exp) | exp | (define name (formals) exp) | (datatype Type {[Con {(field ty)}]}) | (record Record {(field ty)}) | xdef xdef ::= (use file) | (grant name host-spec) | (script Name {site-script}) | (under ({config}) {unit-test}) | unit-test"},
  {"u":"docs.html#definitions","t":"Definitions","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"(define name (formals) body) A function. The formals must be distinct. Top-level names are mutually recursive, and each is defined once, including the names the basis defines. (val name exp) Evaluate once and bind the result. If it fails, nothing is bound and the failure is reported. (datatype Type [Con (field ty)…]…) A sum type: a value is one of its constructors. Constructor names must be distinct, and they share one namespace across the program. (record Record (field ty)…) A product type: a value has all of its fields. (use file) Load another file here, as if its definitions were written at this point. The path is relative to the file that says use. Its definitions, grants and scripts join one global namespace (there are no modules), its top-level expressions run, and its own unit tests run; anything that goes wrong there counts against this file. A file is loaded at most once per run, so a second use of it, including a cycle, does nothing. The whole tree of used files is type-checked before any of it runs. The difference matters when you write code over them. A datatype is consumed with case, one branch per constructor. A record is consumed with (. r field). It also shows up in how you build them: (Sell \"Margins are compressing.\") ; constructor: positional (Message [role User] [content \"FY2025 10-K\"]) ; record: named, in any order"},
  {"u":"docs.html#expressions","t":"Expressions","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":""},
  {"u":"docs.html#exp-core","t":"Core forms","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(if test then else) The test must evaluate to a Boolean; anything else is a checked run-time error. (let* ([x e]…) body) Sequential binding: each right-hand side sees the bindings before it. There is no simultaneous let. (lambda (formals) body) An anonymous function, closing over its environment. (f arg…) Application. Arguments are evaluated left to right. (begin e…) Evaluate in order, answering with the last. Empty begin is #f. Sugar for nested let*. (and e₁ e₂) · (or e₁ e₂) Short-circuiting, and sugar for if. (. e field) Select a field from a record.","s":"Expressions"},
  {"u":"docs.html#exp-case","t":"Case analysis","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"case is how a program consumes a datatype, a list, or a model's answer. If no branch matches, that is a checked run-time error, not a failure. pattern ::= Con ; a constructor with no fields | (Con {name | _}) ; its fields, bound or ignored | '() ; the empty list | (cons name name) ; head and tail | _ ; anything Patterns are flat. A constructor applies to variables or _, never to another pattern, and no variable may repeat within one pattern. To match nested data, nest cases.","k":"case and patterns","s":"Expressions"},
  {"u":"docs.html#exp-build","t":"Building values","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(Con {exp}) ; constructor application Con ; a nullary constructor is just its name (Record {[field exp]}) ; every field, named (list {exp}) ; a list (cons exp exp) ; prepend","s":"Expressions"},
  {"u":"docs.html#effects","t":"The seven agentic forms","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"These are what the language adds. Everything a program can do to the outside world happens through ask, call and consult; there is no other door, which is why a budget, a deadline, the trace and the ownership rules apply to everything."},
  {"u":"docs.html#ask","t":"ask","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(ask model ty context ['site]) Consult a model once, for an answer of type ty. The context is an ordinary list of Messages that you build and pass: nothing accumulates behind your back. The site is a name for this place in the program; scripts and traces are keyed by it, and it defaults to the source location. The answer is validated against ty before it is bound. If it doesn't fit, the ask produces Invalid rather than a value. Asking again is a separate, visible decision — see retry. Before the call, ask reserves the worst case: everything it is about to send, plus the largest answer ty allows. If that doesn't fit the budget, the model is never called and nothing is charged.","s":"The seven agentic forms"},
  {"u":"docs.html#call","t":"call","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(call capability op {exp}) Use a tool once. The capability comes from grant and can only come from there. What the operation answers with is declared by the grant, not written at the call site — see Hosts and capabilities.","s":"The seven agentic forms"},
  {"u":"docs.html#consult","t":"consult","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(consult person ty prompt [propose exp] ['site]) Put a question to a person once, for an answer of type ty. The person comes from a human grant, and ty must be askable, exactly as for ask. The answer is validated the same way. With [propose v], the person sees v as a pre-filled answer and may approve it, edit it, or decline. Approving gives back exactly v, editing gives back the edit, and declining fails with (Declined reason). Run the answer, never the original proposal, and what runs is what was approved. retry never retries Declined. Waiting for a person spends the scope's time, so give a consult's scope room for a person and each model call its own short budget inside it. A person costs no money unless the grant gives a price.","s":"The seven agentic forms"},
  {"u":"docs.html#fail-catch","t":"fail and catch","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(fail exp) (catch exp name handler) A failure is a value, not a crash. catch binds it to name and runs the handler. (fail 'sym) is sugar for (fail (Raised 'sym)). A failure propagates left to right: everything before it still ran and still spent.","s":"The seven agentic forms"},
  {"u":"docs.html#budget","t":"budget","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(budget ([cost exp] [time exp]) body) Everything inside gets at most that much money and time. Either limit may be omitted, which means unlimited. Nesting takes the smaller of the two, and whatever the inside spends is charged to the outside. A budget scope has one pool and one absolute deadline shared by every branch running inside it.","s":"The seven agentic forms"},
  {"u":"docs.html#workflow","t":"workflow and par","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(workflow ([name exp]…) body) (par e₁ e₂) A set of named steps. Dependencies come from the names each step mentions: a step whose inputs are ready starts immediately, alongside the others. The graph must be acyclic, and the names must be distinct — both are checked before the program runs. Steps may not share a stateful tool, because the outcome would depend on timing. Such a workflow is a type error, so the program never starts. To work in parallel on a kernel, fork it first, before the workflow. (A scripted kernel forks freely; a live Python kernel can’t copy its state, so there fork fails with ToolError.) par is sugar for a two-step workflow and answers with a Pair.","s":"The seven agentic forms"},
  {"u":"docs.html#types","t":"Types","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"ty ::= Text | (Text numeral) ; optionally bounded, in bytes | Num | Bool | Sym | Money | Dur | (List ty) | (List ty numeral) | Type | Record ; anything you defined | (Type ty …) ; one with type parameters, applied A datatype or record can take type parameters, and is then given that many arguments wherever it’s used: (datatype (Outcome e a) [Got (value a)] [Unsure (why e)]) (ask m (Outcome (Text 40) Num) ctx) ; a (Got 7), or an (Unsure \"…\") A type variable may appear only inside the definition that declares it, and inside its own definition a type is applied to exactly its own parameters: (Tree a), never (Tree (List a))."},
  {"u":"docs.html#askable","t":"Which types can be asked for","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"A type is askable when it is built only from Text, Num, Bool, Sym, List, and datatypes or records whose fields are all askable, at the arguments given: (Option Num) is askable and (Option Dur) isn’t. Money and Dur are not askable; JSON has no amounts of money or time. That one rule is what keeps authority unforgeable: a capability is not an askable type, so no model answer can ever contain a tool. Authority comes only from the host.","k":"Askability","s":"Types"},
  {"u":"docs.html#bounds","t":"Bounds, and why a type costs money twice","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"A bound caps the answer's size, which caps the reservation. A Verdict with 400-byte reasons reserves far less than the same type with unbounded text, so it fits budgets the unbounded one is refused by. The type is also sent with every ask, as a JSON schema billed as input, and it is usually most of the input. So every constructor and field makes every ask dearer, and no bound on the text inside them helps. In a loop, use prompt caching.","k":"Bounds and cost","s":"Types"},
  {"u":"docs.html#json","t":"How a type maps to JSON","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"µNorman JSON Text, (Text n) a string, at most n bytes when bounded Num an integer Bool true / false Sym a string (List τ), (List τ n) an array, at most n elements a record R an object with exactly R's fields constructor K with fields {\"tag\": \"K\", \"f₁\": …}; a nullary K is {\"tag\": \"K\"}","k":"JSON encoding","s":"Types"},
  {"u":"docs.html#failures","t":"Failures","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"Every failure is a value of the predefined Failure datatype, so you can case on it like anything else. Failure Means (Invalid raw) the reply did not fit the type; raw is what came back (ToolError message) a tool or the provider failed — a 503, a Python NameError (Refused category) the model declined (Declined reason) a person consulted with consult said no OverBudget the reservation did not fit the remaining money PastDeadline the scope's deadline passed (Raised reason) the program's own (fail 'reason) A checked run-time error is a different thing and cannot be caught: an unbound name, an if on a non-Boolean, a case with no matching branch, an exhausted script. Those mean the program is wrong, not that the world misbehaved."},
  {"u":"docs.html#basis","t":"The initial basis","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":""},
  {"u":"docs.html#basis-types","t":"Predefined types","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(datatype Role [System] [User] [Assistant] [Tool]) (record Message (role Role) (content Text)) (datatype Failure [Invalid (raw Text)] [ToolError (message Text)] [Refused (category Sym)] [Declined (reason Text)] [OverBudget] [PastDeadline] [Raised (reason Sym)]) (datatype (Option a) [None] [Some (value a)]) (record (Pair a b) (fst a) (snd b)) (record Resources (cost Money) (time Dur))","s":"The initial basis"},
  {"u":"docs.html#basis-prims","t":"Primitives","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"Primitive Notes + - * / on Num, and on money or durations of the same kind. Scaling takes a Num. = < > comparison; < and > also order strings cons · list list is variadic string-append · string-length length is in bytes show write a value as text that reads back — see below substring · string-truncate · string-contains? byte offsets, as everything else is. substring is a checked error out of range or mid-character; string-truncate is the safe cut. println print a value (remaining) a Resources record for the current scope: (. (remaining) cost) and … time","s":"The initial basis"},
  {"u":"docs.html#show","t":"show","p":"Docs","l":4,"x":"(show v) writes a value as text that reads back. For every askable type it produces exactly the JSON that ask validates, which is what lets you put a value into a prompt and read the answer back out: validate (show v) τ = v for askable τ and v : τ So (show (Sell \"Margins fell.\")) is {\"reason\":\"Margins fell.\",\"tag\":\"Sell\"}. Keys come out sorted, which makes show canonical: equal values always produce identical text. Three things sit outside that law, each on purpose: Money, durations and ∞ are not askable, and JSON has none of them. They are written in µNorman's own literal syntax — $0.50, 40s, ∞ — bare at the top level so a prompt reads naturally, and as strings when nested. Capabilities, models and functions cannot be shown at all. That is a checked run-time error: authority must never reach a prompt, and a function has no written form. The key tag is reserved. A constructor with a field of that name would overwrite its own discriminator, so show refuses rather than quietly producing JSON that means something else.","s":"The initial basis"},
  {"u":"docs.html#basis-functions","t":"Predefined functions","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"These are written in µNorman itself, and each is defined by algebraic laws. (not b) · (<= x y) · (>= x y) The obvious things. (append xs ys) · (length xs) · (filter p xs) · (drop k xs) Lists. drop treats a count of zero or less as zero. (retry n f) Call the thunk up to n+1 times, stopping at the first success. Retries Invalid and ToolError only — never OverBudget or PastDeadline, since asking again cannot help and would spend what little is left. (repair n f ctx) Like retry, but the bad reply and a correction are appended to the context. Costs more per attempt, because the context grows — a trade-off explicit context makes visible. (best-of k f check) k attempts, concurrently, keeping one that check accepts. Built from a recursive par, so the cost adds up and the time is the longest attempt. (window n ctx) A context policy: every System message, then the last n others. Note that it moves system messages to the front. These use helpers that are also top-level names, so a program can't define them again: attempt, best-of*, correction, first-some, last-n and system?.","s":"The initial basis"},
  {"u":"docs.html#hosts","t":"Hosts and capabilities","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"(grant name (model [id \"…\"] [in p] [out p] [cache 5min|1h] [cache-read p] [ceiling n] [think n])) (grant name (kernel {[op ty]})) ; stateful (grant name (filesystem {[op ty]})) ; stateful (grant name (http {[op ty]})) ; stateless (grant name (human [price money])) ; a person, for consult; the price is optional Live, only model and kernel (with --python) have real hosts so far; filesystem and http answer only from scripts. A human grant must be bound on the command line before a live run starts: --human name=tty asks at the terminal, and --human name=unattended makes any consult stop the run."},
  {"u":"docs.html#tool-types","t":"What a tool answers with","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"A grant declares each operation's result type, so a tool's answer is typed the same way a model's is. The call site names no type. (grant py (kernel [exec (Text 4000)] [count Num] [solve Outcome])) (call py count \"wc -l\") ; a Num, not the text \"7\" A grant with no declarations is open: any operation, result Text. A grant with declarations is closed: only those operations, plus fork on a kernel. Calling another is a checked run-time error — the program is wrong, not the world. A declared Text takes the result verbatim, and only its bound is checked. Any other type reads the result as JSON and validates it, exactly as a model's reply is validated. Failure is Invalid, so retry handles a malformed tool result for free. Note the difference from ask: there, Text is a JSON string; here, it is the tool's raw output. Every model field is optional and falls back to a default. Prices are dollars per million tokens, with up to three decimals: [in 3] is $3 per million input tokens, [in 0.1] is ten cents. Prices are stated here, not looked up — the API does not report them, so a grant is where the program says what it believes a token costs. If they are wrong, the budget arithmetic is wrong. [cache 5min] or [cache 1h] turns on prompt caching. An answer type's schema is sent as input with every ask and is usually most of it, so a loop that asks again and again pays for it again and again. With caching, a repeated prefix is read back at [cache-read], a tenth of [in] unless the grant says otherwise. Writing the cache costs a quarter more than plain input (twice as much for the hour), so a one-off ask costs more with caching, not less, and a caching grant reserves input at the write price. Use it where asks repeat: an agent loop, retry, repair. Scripted runs simulate the cache, so a test's costs are what a live run would pay.","s":"Hosts and capabilities"},
  {"u":"docs.html#testing","t":"Scripts and tests","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"Tests run against scripts: canned replies keyed by site, with a simulated clock. Every test runs in a fresh world — a fresh pool, a fresh deadline, the script rewound, an empty trace — so tests are independent and exactly repeatable. (script Name [site (reply \"…\") (out n) (latency d) (reply \"…\") (out n) (latency d)] ; consumed in order [cap/op (result \"…\") (latency d)]) ; a tool, keyed capability/operation Faults are injected the same way, which is what makes failure paths testable at all: (reply \"…\") what the model says; pair with (out n) for output tokens (refusal \"category\") the model declines (provider-error \"503 …\") the provider fails (result \"…\") a tool succeeds (error \"…\") a tool fails (approve) a person approves the proposal unchanged (answer \"…\") a person gives this answer, as JSON (decline \"…\") a person declines, with this reason (latency 2s) how long it takes on the simulated clock"},
  {"u":"docs.html#checks","t":"Test forms","p":"Docs","l":3,"x":"(under ([script Name] [cost $1.00] [time 1min]) {unit-test}) (check-expect e expected) Both sides run in their own fresh world, so evaluating the expected value cannot consume the script. Two equal failures agree too, so (check-expect (ask-v) (fail (Refused 'cyber))) says exactly how it fails. (check-assert e) The expression is true. (check-fail e pattern) It fails, with a failure matching the pattern: (check-fail (ask-v) (Invalid _)). (check-error e) It is a checked run-time error — the program is wrong, not the world. (check-type-error e) It is ill-typed, so the checker rejects it before it runs. Every other test fails if its expression is ill-typed. (check-within e ([cost c] [time t])) It spends at most c and takes at most t, whatever its result. That lets you measure failures as well as successes. (check-equiv e₁ e₂ 'grade) Two expressions agree, at 'exact (result and the whole world, trace included), 'resource (result, spend, time, host state), or 'value. A complete, runnable example of all of it together:","s":"Scripts and tests"},
  {"u":"docs.html#sugar","t":"Syntactic sugar","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"Sugar is defined by translation, so it inherits every theorem already proved about the core forms, with no new cases to check. (let* () e) = e (let* ([x e₁] rest…) e) = LETX(x, e₁, (let* (rest…) e)) (begin) = #f (begin e₁ … eₙ) = (let* ([_₁ e₁] …) eₙ) (par e₁ e₂) = (workflow ([a e₁] [b e₂]) (Pair [fst a] [snd b])) (and e₁ e₂) = (if e₁ e₂ #f) (or e₁ e₂) = (if e₁ #t e₂) (fail 'sym) = (fail (Raised 'sym)) (ask m τ c) = (ask m τ c 'file:line:col) (budget ([cost e]) body) = BUDGET(e, ∞, body)"},
  {"u":"docs.html#wellformed","t":"What is checked before a program runs","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"A whole file is checked before any of it runs, and a program that doesn't check never starts, so a mistake found here can't spend money. There are two passes. The parser rejects what is decidable from the syntax alone: The formals of a lambda or define are distinct; so are a datatype's constructors and a record's fields. A workflow's binding names are distinct. A workflow's dependency graph is acyclic. Patterns are flat, and no variable repeats within one pattern. Forms are applied with the right number of parts. The type and effect checker then infers every type, with no annotations, and rejects a program that could reach any of these: an if on a non-Boolean; applying a non-function, or with the wrong number of arguments; an unbound name; an unknown constructor, record or field; ask on a non-model, consult on a non-person, call on a non-capability; an unaskable type in ask or consult, or a proposal of the wrong shape; an undeclared operation on a closed grant; show of a function, model, person or capability; fail of a non-Failure; two concurrent workflow steps that share a stateful tool. It warns, without rejecting, about a case that may not match, a workflow step that reads (remaining), and two steps that consult the same person. Some things stay run-time errors by design: division by zero and overflow, a substring out of range, a size bound exceeded, an exhausted script, and the step limit. norman --no-check runs a program without the checker."},
  {"u":"docs.html#reserved","t":"Reserved words","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"val define datatype record use grant script under check-expect check-assert check-error check-fail check-within check-equiv check-type-error if let* lambda case ask call consult fail catch budget workflow begin par and or _ cost, time, propose and price are not reserved: they appear only in fixed bracket positions, so they are free to use as names. cost and time are the fields of Resources."},
  {"u":"docs.html#deeper","t":"Going deeper","p":"Docs","l":2,"x":"The formal definition — abstract syntax, big-step semantics, every rule, and the theorems. The machine — the event-by-event semantics behind concurrency and shared budgets. The laws — which refactors are safe, which are not, and the nine problems writing them found."},
  {"u":"./","t":"µNorman","p":"About","l":1,"x":"A small language for AI agents Typed model calls, budgets in money and time, tools, and concurrency."},
  {"u":"./#why","t":"Background","p":"About","l":2,"x":"Different studies and previous works inspired the idea for µNorman. They all identified shortcomings and existing problems with current agentic work: Problem What µNorman does Unreliable answers every ask names the type of answer it wants Decisions answers can be choices that the program branches on Cost and deadlines money and time are built in, and the budget is never overspent Latency each budget has one shared clock with deadlines Failed steps failures are values, and retry wraps a single step Tool access tools are capabilities that a model can never create Types, and which tools concurrent steps may share, are checked before a program runs; budgets and deadlines are enforced while it runs. See Status."},
  {"u":"./#tour","t":"An overview of the main capabilities of µNorman:","p":"About","l":2,"x":""},
  {"u":"./#tour-ask","t":"1. Ask","p":"About","l":3,"x":"You give ask a model and the context it should see. You also name the type of answer you want. If the reply fits that type, you get a real value back. If it doesn’t, the ask fails. The context is just a list you build yourself. Nothing gets added to it without you knowing.","s":"An overview of the main capabilities of µNorman:"},
  {"u":"./#tour-failure","t":"2. Handling Failure","p":"About","l":3,"x":"Agents fail a lot. The reply might not fit the type. The provider might be down. You might run out of money or time. In each case ask gives back a failure instead of crashing. There are six kinds: Invalid, ToolError, Refused, OverBudget, PastDeadline and Raised. You can catch any of them and decide what to do next.","s":"An overview of the main capabilities of µNorman:"},
  {"u":"./#tour-budget","t":"3. The budget can never be overspent","p":"About","l":3,"x":"Before it calls the model, ask reserves the worst case. That means the full prompt plus the longest answer the type allows. If that doesn’t fit in the budget, the model is never called and you pay nothing. This is why types have size limits. Leave the limit off and the same question gets turned away.","s":"An overview of the main capabilities of µNorman:"},
  {"u":"./#tour-loop","t":"4. Agent Loop","p":"About","l":3,"x":"This is the CP-Agent design. The model picks the next step. If it writes code, we run it and show it the output. When it says it’s done, we stop. The answer is a choice, so the loop is a plain case. The budget around it makes sure it always ends.","s":"An overview of the main capabilities of µNorman:"},
  {"u":"./#tour-concurrency","t":"5. Concurrency","p":"About","l":3,"x":"A workflow is a set of named steps. A step waits only on the steps it mentions by name. As soon as its inputs are ready, it runs. In the example below, four model calls take 40 seconds this way. Forcing them into “run two, wait, run two” takes 60 seconds. Both cost the same. Running things at once saves time, not money.","s":"An overview of the main capabilities of µNorman:"},
  {"u":"./#tour-laws","t":"6. Laws","p":"About","l":3,"x":"Every construct comes with algebraic laws. They tell you which rewrites are safe. The non-laws matter just as much. A compiler might merge two identical asks into one. That would change the result, since the same question can get a different answer each time. So µNorman never caches a model’s answer on its own.","s":"An overview of the main capabilities of µNorman:"},
  {"u":"./#guarantees","t":"What µNorman guarantees","p":"About","l":2,"x":"The budget is never overspent, even with many steps running at once. The interpreter checks this invariant in every state it reaches. A model can’t hand your program a tool. Authority comes only from the host. No answer is accepted after the deadline. Concurrency doesn’t change answers. A workflow gives the same result as running its steps one at a time, when no budget or deadline binds and no step reads its own remaining budget. Every model call and tool call is recorded in a trace. That is what makes testing, replay and cost profiling possible."},
  {"u":"./#status","t":"Status","p":"About","l":2,"x":"µNorman is a research prototype, designed in the style of Norman Ramsey’s Programming Languages: Build, Prove, and Compare and his Seven Lessons in Program Design."},
  {"u":"./#status","t":"What works","p":"About","l":3,"x":"The language, its semantics, its laws, and an interpreter that runs them. 291 unit tests written in µNorman, 17 laws checked on 150 random scripts each, a type checker tested on 400 random programs, and a suite of deliberately wrong tests that must fail.","s":"Status"},
  {"u":"./#status","t":"It runs!","p":"About","l":3,"x":"CP-Agent solves problems against Claude Sonnet 5 with a Python kernel. The traces are here, including the budget refusing to start a run it couldn’t afford.","s":"Status"},
  {"u":"./#status","t":"Checked before it runs","p":"About","l":3,"x":"Types and effects are inferred, with no annotations, and a program that doesn’t type-check never starts, so a type mistake can’t spend money. Sharing a stateful tool between concurrent steps is a type error too.","s":"Status"},
  {"u":"./#status","t":"A small standard library","p":"About","l":3,"x":"show writes any value as text that reads back, and there are substrings, so a program can build real prompts. It is still small: no sorting, no maps, no regular expressions.","s":"Status"},
  {"u":"./#status","t":"Performance is a hypothesis","p":"About","l":3,"x":"µNorman makes an agent’s behaviour knowable and bounded. It does not make the model better, and nothing here has been measured against a baseline. One Important Finding The answer’s type is sent with every ask as a JSON schema, which accounts for a significant portion of the input tokens. A type costs money twice: bounding an answer shrinks the reservation, but adding a constructor or a field makes every ask dearer on input, for as long as the program runs. That falsified one of the algebraic laws, which had priced an ask without its schema.","s":"Status"},
  {"u":"./#more","t":"This is cool, what can I look at next?","p":"About","l":2,"x":"Write and run your own µNorman The language guide The formal definition The laws Claude's reading notes"},
  {"u":"demo.html","t":"Demo","p":"Demo","l":1,"x":"CP-Agent is a simple agent loop from Stefan Szeider’s work on agentic constraint programming. I wrote it in µNorman and ran it against Claude Sonnet 5 with a Python process. Ask the model→ Run its Python→ Show it the output↺ Stop when it says Done"},
  {"u":"demo.html#results","t":"Results","p":"Demo","l":2,"x":"Task Budget Spent Result Sum of multiples $0.50 $0.0027 in 4.7s 233168 Chickens and cows $0.50 $0.0041 in 4.9s 23 chickens, 7 cows Same task, tiny budget $0.002 $0.00 OverBudget"},
  {"u":"demo.html#traces","t":"Traces","p":"Demo","l":2,"x":"µNorman logs every model call (ask) and tool call (call) with its tokens, cost and timing."},
  {"u":"demo.html#run-1","t":"Sum of multiples","p":"Demo","l":3,"x":"One piece of code, one run, then the answer. The second ask is bigger because it carries the code and its output.","s":"Traces"},
  {"u":"demo.html#run-2","t":"Chickens and cows","p":"Demo","l":3,"x":"I asked it to check its work. It solved and checked in a single piece of code.","s":"Traces"},
  {"u":"demo.html#run-3","t":"Same task, tiny budget","p":"Demo","l":3,"x":"The “$0.50 budget” label in the summary line was a bug in my test report. The real cap was $0.002. The model was never called. Each step reserves its worst case first, which is about $0.04 here. That doesn’t fit in $0.002, so the run stops before sending anything. The same check runs before every ask and call, so it can’t be skipped.","s":"Traces"},
  {"u":"demo.html#same-program","t":"Try it in your browser","p":"Demo","l":2,"x":"This is the program behind the traces. Here a script stands in for the model and Python, so it runs free and offline. The last test shows a run that stops partway when the money runs low."},
  {"u":"demo.html#yourself","t":"Run it yourself","p":"Demo","l":2,"x":"Scripted tests, free with no API key: cargo run -- examples/cp-agent.nrm Live runs need ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and Python on your PATH. Each costs a few cents: cargo test --test live_cp_agent -- --ignored --nocapture cargo run -- --live --python python3 --trace examples/cp-agent.nrm No sandbox The kernel runs whatever code the model writes, on your machine. Use a container or VM. LIVE-SETUP.md covers API keys and spending caps."},
  {"u":"playground.html","t":"Playground","p":"Playground","l":1,"x":"The whole interpreter runs here, compiled to WebAssembly. Models answer from scripts, so programs are deterministic and free, and nothing leaves your machine. Press Run, or Ctrl/⌘ + Enter. Example Result Not run yet."},
  {"u":"playground.html#cheatsheet","t":"Cheat Sheet","p":"Playground","l":2,"x":""},
  {"u":"playground.html#cheat-definitions","t":"Definitions and types","p":"Playground","l":3,"x":"(define f (x y) e) a function (val x e) a binding (datatype T [K (f τ)] …) a sum type: a value is one of the Ks (record R (f τ) …) a record; read a field with (. r f) (Text 400), Num, Bool, Sym, (List τ 8) the askable types; bounds make answers cheaper to reserve","s":"Cheat Sheet"},
  {"u":"playground.html#cheat-forms","t":"The seven agentic forms","p":"Playground","l":3,"x":"(ask m τ ctx 'site) consult a model once, for an answer of type τ (call κ op args…) use a tool once (consult h τ prompt [propose v] 'site) ask a granted person once; approving gives back exactly v, declining fails with Declined (fail φ) / (catch e x h) raise and handle a failure (budget ([cost $1.00] [time 1min]) e) a money and time limit for everything inside (workflow ([x e] …) body) named steps; dependencies come from the names each uses (par e₁ e₂) sugar for a two-step workflow, returning a Pair","s":"Cheat Sheet"},
  {"u":"playground.html#cheat-values","t":"Text and values","p":"Playground","l":3,"x":"(show v) write a value as text that reads back: validate (show v) τ = v (string-append a b …) · (string-length s) length is in bytes (substring s i j) byte offsets; a checked error out of range or mid-character (string-truncate s n) the safe cut: at most n bytes, never splitting a character (string-contains? s sub) a predicate","s":"Cheat Sheet"},
  {"u":"playground.html#cheat-failures","t":"Failures","p":"Playground","l":3,"x":"Invalid (the reply didn’t fit the type) · ToolError · Refused · OverBudget · PastDeadline · Raised. retry handles the first two and never the budget ones.","s":"Cheat Sheet"},
  {"u":"playground.html#cheat-tests","t":"Scripts and tests","p":"Playground","l":3,"x":"(grant m (model [in 3] [out 15] [ceiling 8000])) a model, priced in µ$ per token (grant py (kernel)) a stateful tool (script s [site (reply \"…\") (out 20) (latency 2s)]) canned replies, keyed by site (provider-error \"503 …\"), (refusal \"…\"), (error \"…\") injected faults (under ([script s] [cost $1.00] [time 1min]) …) the world each test runs in check-expect · check-fail · check-within · check-equiv · check-error · check-type-error · check-assert the unit-test forms Two things that will trip you up Constructors take positional arguments ((Sell \"reason\")) while records take named ones ((Message [role User] [content \"hi\"])). And to put a value into a prompt, use (show v): it writes the same JSON that ask validates, so what you write out reads back in.","s":"Cheat Sheet"},
];
